gst-interpipe: fix crashes when a node is torn down while listeners are active

Applies on top of games-on-whales/gst-interpipe 0c454917cb3cf4f2b2f2729f5f72a4dee072c7c9
(GST_INTERPIPE_REF in docker/wolf.Dockerfile). Wolf ticket XERK-2114.

Use-after-free races when an interpipesink (node) is destroyed while
interpipesrc listeners on other threads start, stop, or forward events:

1. gst_inter_pipe_add_node / gst_inter_pipe_remove_node walked the global
   listeners table without listeners_mutex while listen_node / leave_node
   inserted and freed entries (SIGSEGV in gst_inter_pipe_ilistener_node_removed
   <- gst_inter_pipe_sink_finalize). They now hold listeners_mutex (made
   recursive: the callbacks re-enter listen_node / leave_node) and iterate a
   snapshot of the listeners.
2. listen_node / leave_node could call into a sink that was mid-teardown. The
   sink now unregisters in dispose, before its pads are released, and removal
   is serialized with listen/leave by listeners_mutex.
3. interpipesrc forwarded upstream events / context queries to a raw node
   pointer with no lock. Forwards now pin the node (in-flight count under
   nodes_mutex) and remove_node waits for them. No lock is held while
   forwarding: holding listeners_mutex there deadlocks against a producer
   streaming thread blocked on a consumer's preroll.
4. A sink whose name was already taken (add_node failed) still unregistered
   that name on dispose, removing the live owner and orphaning its listeners
   (use-after-free on push). remove_node now only removes the entry if it is
   the given node, and a sink that failed to register forgets its node name.
5. interpipesrc's listen-to string was freed by the setter without the
   listeners lock while node_added / node_removed, start / stop and the
   registry's own entry (which aliased it) still used it (use-after-free when
   a consumer switches producers while it or a producer starts or stops). The
   setter, getter, start and stop now run under the listeners lock
   (gst_inter_pipe_lock_listeners), and the registry keeps its own copy.
6. interpipesrc's pending serial events queue was pushed by the node's
   streaming thread and popped by the listener's own streaming thread with no
   lock (SIGSEGV in g_queue_push_tail <- gst_inter_pipe_ilistener_push_event).
   It now has its own mutex; the event is pushed downstream outside it.

